+// NOTE: deliberately NO POLICY_EXPORT on the three functions below, matching
+// how upstream declares GetPolicyVerificationKey(). :common_constants is a
+// source_set carrying the component_implementation config, so EVERY module that
+// links it would dllexport these -- and several do (content.dll and
+// chrome/browser both pull it in). lld-link then sees the symbol defined in the
+// module's own cloud_policy_constants.obj AND imported from content.dll, and
+// fails the component build with "duplicate symbol". Without the macro each
+// linking module simply gets its own private copy, which is correct here: these
+// are pure functions over baked-in constants, and every caller's target already
+// deps :common_constants directly. Adding the macro is not a portability fix --
+// it is what breaks Windows. (It went unnoticed on macOS: there the attribute
+// only widens dylib visibility, and the mach-o linker resolves the duplicate
+// silently instead of erroring.)
+
 // Public half of the verification key that is used to verify that policy
-// signing keys are originating from DM server.
+// signing keys are originating from DM server. This is the PRIMARY root; see
+// GetPolicyVerificationKeys() for the full trusted set.
 std::string GetPolicyVerificationKey();
 
+// Every root this build trusts, primary first. A release build carries a
+// dormant recovery root as a second element; dev and staging carry one. A
+// signature is accepted when ANY element verifies it -- which is also why this
+// set does not revoke anything: dropping a compromised root requires shipping a
+// build whose set no longer contains it.
+std::vector<std::string> GetPolicyVerificationKeys();
+
+// Whether `key` (a DER SubjectPublicKeyInfo) is one of the trusted roots.
+// Used for cached-key rotation decisions, where the question is membership --
+// "was this cache endorsed by something we still trust" -- and not equality
+// with the primary, which would call every recovery-root-endorsed cache stale.
+bool IsKnownVerificationKey(std::string_view key);
+
 // Corresponding hash.
 extern const char kPolicyVerificationKeyHash[];
 
